The difficulty is that it is not clear that the functor
\[\widetilde C\longrightarrow W^{-1}C\]
above is also \uline{faithful} (which would imply that it is an equivalence of categories \inserted{and even an iso}). \deleted{In this case \unsure{the inverse functor would necessarily be given by}} \inserted{It amounts to the $=$ to say} that the can functor $C\mathbin{-}\widetilde C$ (whose composite with $\widetilde C\mathbin{-}W^{-1}C$ is indeed the can. functor $C\mathbin{-}W^{-1}C$) transforms arrows of $W$ into iso, or again that the construction we have just \unsure{considered}, for \deleted{the case} \inserted{finding} an inverse of $[f,i]$ in $\widetilde C$, is indeed valid, \deleted{\unsure{which}}
This \unsure{would imply} moreover \inserted{it seems,} that, \deleted{the} putting $W_0=W\cap\mathrm{cof}$, the functor
\[W_0^{-1}C\longrightarrow W^{-1}C\]
would be an equivalence of categories, for
\[\widetilde C\longrightarrow W^{-1}C\]
visibly factors through
\diagram{\begin{tikzcd}
\widetilde C \arrow[r] \arrow[d,phantom,"\unsure{\simeq}"] & W_0^{-1}C \arrow[r]& W^{-1}C\\
W^{-1}C&&
\end{tikzcd}}
But I doubt that it suffices to invert the \uline{cofibrant} weak equiv., for \unsure{all} the weak equiv. to become invertible !

I therefore correct my aim, by taking instead the category (now denoted by $\widetilde C$) having $\unsure{=^{\mathrm{s}}}$ objects as $C$, \deleted{\unsure{and thus defined by the arrows from $x\longrightarrow y$}} \inserted{and \unsure{having as} the $\pi_0$ of the $\uline{\mathrm{Ch}}_0(x,y)$,} \deleted{\unsure{now defined}} \inserted{this category} (for given $x,y$) as that of diagrams
\diagram{\begin{tikzcd}
\deleted{\unsure{x}} \arrow[r,phantom,"\deleted{\unsure{\longrightarrow}}"]&\deleted{\unsure{y}}
\end{tikzcd}}
\diagram{\begin{tikzcd}
x \arrow[dr,hook,"f"']&y\arrow[d,"i"',"\sim"]\\
&\bar y
\end{tikzcd}}
with $\left\{\begin{array}{l}f\in\mathrm{cof}\\i\in W\end{array}\right.$
